<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna of East Anglia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 654</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna was king of the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angles">Angles</a> from about 636 until 654, a devout Christian ruler remembered for his saintly daughters. He gave refuge to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cenwalh-of-wessex">Cenwalh of Wessex</a> and was killed in battle at Bulcamp by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a>. His daughters included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelthryth">Æthelthryth</a>, founder of the abbey of Ely, and Seaxburh, abbess of Ely after her.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-of-east-anglia.jpg" alt="Anna of East Anglia" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Eileen Henderson · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marshland_near_Blythburgh_-_geograph.org.uk_-_192171.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
